1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160
A frontier language model serving

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:07,280
production traffic produces each output

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:09,240
token in roughly 30 milliseconds.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:11,400
Producing those tokens, turning a

5
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:14,280
trained model into a service that

6
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:15,840
actually responds to users, is what LLM

7
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,640
inference means.

8
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:19,920
It is the engineering discipline that

9
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:21,720
sits between a model checkpoint and a

10
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:23,680
chat window.

11
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:26,200
Requests come in through a routing layer

12
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:28,080
that picks which machine handles yours,

13
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:29,800
and a scheduler batches them alongside

14
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,800
thousands of others.

15
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,920
A memory manager decides which weights

16
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,160
live on which chip, while kernels

17
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,880
perform the actual arithmetic, and

18
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,920
orchestration layer wires it all

19
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,760
together.

20
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:44,240
The scale of this problem caught me off

21
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:46,640
guard. Deloitte projects that inference

22
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,960
will account for roughly 2/3 of all AI

23
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:51,360
compute in 2026.

24
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,360
And the five largest US hyperscalers

25
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,320
have guided to combined AI capital

26
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:58,560
expenditure in the $700 billion range

27
00:00:56,320 --> 00:01:00,720
this year, with inference consuming the

28
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:03,440
majority of that spend. [music]

29
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:06,240
OpenAI's API alone processes more than

30
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:07,440
20 trillion tokens every day.

31
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,360
At that volume, [music]

32
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:11,800
every architectural decision in this

33
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,440
space, from chip layout to memory

34
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:18,400
hierarchy to scheduling policy, comes

35
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:20,400
down to one number, cost per token.

36
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:21,920
The models keep growing.

37
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,240
The frontier systems running in

38
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:26,320
production today have parameter counts

39
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:28,000
in the high hundreds of billions to over

40
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:30,840
a trillion.

41
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,720
Open systems like Chimera 2 and DeepSeek

42
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:37,080
V4, and their closed source counterparts

43
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:39,120
at OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google are the

44
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,320
workloads engineers actually have to

45
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,280
serve.

46
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:44,960
Running models at that scale in

47
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:47,240
real-time at a cost that holds up

48
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:50,240
commercially requires a stack of

49
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:52,360
techniques that mostly did not exist 5

50
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:54,520
years ago.

51
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:57,320
In this series, we work through that

52
00:01:54,520 --> 00:02:00,080
stack from first principles. We'll trace

53
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:02,160
a single token end to end.

54
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:03,640
From the moment a user presses enter,

55
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:06,280
through the routing layer, the

56
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:08,039
scheduler, the prefill cluster, the

57
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:10,960
key-value cache transfer between

58
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:13,640
machines, the decode cluster, the

59
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,720
multi-token decoding loop, and back to

60
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:18,000
the user's screen.

61
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:19,080
Those terms will all become clear as we

62
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,840
go.

63
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:23,360
At each [music] stage, we'll work out

64
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:26,080
what the engineering problem is and why

65
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:28,280
the field made the choices it did.

66
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:30,880
By the end, you should be able to look

67
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:34,760
at any production inference system and

68
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:37,480
explain why each component is there.

69
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:39,440
Each token takes 30 milliseconds. Where

70
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:41,920
does the time go?

71
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:43,680
A modern AI accelerator can perform

72
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:46,400
around a quadrillion arithmetic

73
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:48,760
operations per second, a trillion in

74
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:50,600
every single millisecond.

75
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:52,640
Producing one token from a typical

76
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:55,680
frontier model requires on the order of

77
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:58,120
a hundred billion arithmetic operations.

78
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:00,160
At a quadrillion operations per second,

79
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:01,920
that's about a tenth of a millisecond of

80
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,440
pure math.

81
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:07,440
The chip could, in principle, finish the

82
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:10,560
math for 300 more tokens in the rest of

83
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:12,320
those 30 milliseconds.

84
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:14,360
It doesn't.

85
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:16,760
Each token consumes the full 30

86
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:18,640
milliseconds.

87
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:21,160
That [music] gap between what the

88
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:24,480
arithmetic implies and what actually

89
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:25,760
happens is the central question of LLM

90
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:29,160
inference.

91
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:31,519
Of those 30 milliseconds, less than 1%

92
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:35,880
is spent on arithmetic.

93
00:03:31,519 --> 00:03:37,840
The other 99% is something else.

94
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:40,640
By the end of this video, we'll know

95
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:42,440
what that 99% is.

96
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:45,040
And we'll be able to derive those 30

97
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:47,959
milliseconds from just two numbers for

98
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:51,000
any model on any chip.

99
00:03:47,959 --> 00:03:53,160
A modern AI accelerator has two numbers

100
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,800
that determine how fast it can produce

101
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:56,640
tokens.

102
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:59,440
Compute throughput, measured in

103
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:02,640
floating-point operations per second, is

104
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:05,400
how fast the chip can do arithmetic.

105
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:09,240
Memory bandwidth, measured in bytes per

106
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:11,400
second, is how fast it can move data

107
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:14,000
between its off-chip memory and the

108
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:16,280
chip's arithmetic units.

109
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,840
We care about both because producing a

110
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:20,880
single token requires both: fetching

111
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:22,919
weights from memory, then multiplying

112
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:25,080
them against the input. If either

113
00:04:22,919 --> 00:04:26,560
channel is too slow, the other sits

114
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:28,880
idle.

115
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:30,760
These two specs scale on completely

116
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,680
different physics.

117
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:33,880
Compute throughput tracks transistor

118
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:36,120
density,

119
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:38,919
how many arithmetic units you can fit on

120
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:41,400
a single chip. And that number has grown

121
00:04:38,919 --> 00:04:43,800
exponentially for decades.

122
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:45,480
Memory bandwidth is gated by the product

123
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:48,240
of three things:

124
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:50,560
how fast you can signal [music] across

125
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:53,760
each pin on the package,

126
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:56,040
how many pins each memory stack uses,

127
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:58,760
and how many memory stacks you can

128
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:01,160
physically fit around the chip.

129
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:03,480
Each of those three terms has its own

130
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:06,840
hard physical ceiling.

131
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:08,600
Transistor density has not hit one yet.

132
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:10,840
When I first looked at how these two

133
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:12,520
specs have tracked over the last decade,

134
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:14,240
the divergence was larger than I

135
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:17,280
expected.

136
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:19,400
Between 2012 and 2022, the

137
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,680
floating-point throughput of top-tier

138
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,919
server GPUs grew by a factor of about

139
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:26,320
80.

140
00:05:23,919 --> 00:05:28,919
Over the same decade, off-chip memory

141
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:31,240
bandwidth on those same GPUs grew by a

142
00:05:28,919 --> 00:05:35,160
factor of about 17.

143
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:37,640
A 4.7x divergence in one decade. The

144
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:40,000
curves are still spreading.

145
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:41,040
Computer architects call this the memory

146
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,480
wall.

147
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:45,840
Wolf and McKee coined the term in 1995,

148
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:47,920
more than 20 years before the

149
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:50,200
transformer existed.

150
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:51,920
The gap is built into the physics. It is

151
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:55,240
not closing.

152
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:57,720
What is new is the workload.

153
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:00,000
Large language model inference is among

154
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:02,320
the most bite-hungry computations ever

155
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,480
built to run on this hardware.

156
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,000
So, the memory wall, which has always

157
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,920
been a concern in computer architecture,

158
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,560
has become the dominant concern.

159
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:15,760
On a modern accelerator, arithmetic is

160
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:17,520
cheap and data movement is expensive.

161
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:19,880
Every architectural decision in

162
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:22,240
production inference, every paper we

163
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:24,919
will look at in this series, takes that

164
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:26,880
asymmetry as given.

165
00:06:24,919 --> 00:06:29,000
So, arithmetic is cheap and data

166
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:31,160
movement is expensive.

167
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,840
To see where that bites us, we need to

168
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:35,240
trace what actually [music] happens

169
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:37,080
inside the chip when it runs a language

170
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:39,680
model, because the structure of a

171
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:42,400
transformer is what makes inference so

172
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:44,400
hungry for memory bandwidth.

173
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:46,600
A language model takes a piece of text

174
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:48,520
and produces the next token.

175
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:50,880
Tokens are the units of text the model

176
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:53,520
works with, slightly smaller than words

177
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:55,800
on average. So, a 500-word prompt

178
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,280
typically becomes somewhere between 600

179
00:06:55,800 --> 00:07:00,240
and 800 tokens.

180
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:02,760
Each token first gets mapped to a

181
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:05,080
vector, a fixed-length list of numbers

182
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:07,480
several thousand dimensions wide, that

183
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:10,240
encodes both the token's identity and

184
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:11,919
its position in the sequence.

185
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:13,480
Those vectors then enter a stack of

186
00:07:11,919 --> 00:07:15,440
repeated processing units called

187
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:17,520
transformer layers.

188
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:20,960
A modern large model has anywhere from

189
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:23,840
60 to over a hundred of these layers.

190
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:25,480
Every layer performs two operations in

191
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:27,720
order.

192
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:29,680
The first is attention.

193
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:31,800
The model takes each token's current

194
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:33,760
vector and projects it into three

195
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:37,400
separate vectors.

196
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:39,240
A query, a key, and a value.

197
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:41,520
The query captures what this token is

198
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:43,920
looking for in the sequence.

199
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:45,720
The key captures what it can offer to

200
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:47,240
other tokens.

201
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:49,400
And the value is what it actually

202
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:51,760
contributes when matched.

203
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:53,960
The model compares every query against

204
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:56,360
every key, producing a matrix of

205
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:58,320
similarity scores, then uses those

206
00:07:56,360 --> 00:08:00,960
scores to blend the values into an

207
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:02,640
updated vector for each position.

208
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:04,480
That is how context from one token

209
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:06,480
reaches another.

210
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:08,000
The second operation is the feed forward

211
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:10,240
network.

212
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,600
Each token's vector, independently of

213
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:15,080
the others, gets multiplied through two

214
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:17,240
large weight matrices with a non-linear

215
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:18,960
activation between them.

216
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:21,440
This is where the model applies what it

217
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:23,800
learned during training, transforming

218
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:25,520
each token's representation without

219
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:27,360
reference to the other tokens in the

220
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:29,440
sequence.

221
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:30,840
The output of one layer is the input to

222
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:33,000
the next.

223
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:34,719
After all the layers have run, the model

224
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:37,159
takes the final vector at the last

225
00:08:34,719 --> 00:08:39,560
position in the sequence, multiplies it

226
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:42,039
against a vocabulary projection matrix

227
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:43,760
that scores every possible next token,

228
00:08:42,039 --> 00:08:46,040
and picks one.

229
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:48,520
That is the output.

230
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:50,600
That entire sequence, from the input

231
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:53,320
vectors through every layer to the

232
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:55,440
sampled output token, is one forward

233
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:57,360
pass.

234
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:59,400
This is the architecture you already

235
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:01,839
have in your head if you have read the

236
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:03,600
original Transformer paper.

237
00:09:01,839 --> 00:09:05,760
It is correct.

238
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:08,200
What I want to focus on is the one

239
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:10,760
feature of it that ends up dominating

240
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,680
the rest of this discussion.

241
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:15,640
Every one of those layers is

242
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:17,800
parameterized by weight matrices.

243
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:20,280
The attention step has matrices that

244
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,800
produce queries, keys, and values from

245
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:25,520
each token's vector, plus a matrix that

246
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:28,200
combines the attention output.

247
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:30,240
The feed forward step has two more.

248
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:33,360
Those weights are what the model learned

249
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:36,160
during training. They define the model.

250
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:40,120
For a 70 billion parameter model, those

251
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:42,080
weights occupy about 140 GB of memory if

252
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:43,240
each weight is stored in 16-bits

253
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:46,200
precision.

254
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:49,320
2 bytes per weight, 70 billion weights,

255
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:51,080
140 GB total.

256
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:54,040
The weights live in the chip's main

257
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:56,320
off-chip memory pool, generally HBM,

258
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:59,331
high bandwidth memory. A stack of

259
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:01,280
silicon memory chips called dies bonded

260
00:09:59,331 --> 00:10:02,520
[music] next to the GPU die on the same

261
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:05,720
package

262
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:07,480
connected through a wide parallel bus.

263
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:10,680
Every time the model runs a forward

264
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:13,320
pass, all 140 GB of weights have to be

265
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:16,400
read out of HBM and moved across that

266
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:18,200
bus into the chip's arithmetic units.

267
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:19,800
The weights do not change between

268
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:22,120
requests.

269
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:25,280
They are the model.

270
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:29,080
They sit in HBM and every forward pass

271
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:31,120
pulls all of them across the memory bus.

272
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:33,480
Every forward pass drags all the weights

273
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:35,480
across the memory bus.

274
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:37,520
Not every forward pass does the same

275
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:39,120
amount of work with them.

276
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:41,560
When you send a prompt to a language

277
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:43,440
model, the system processes that request

278
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:45,880
in two phases.

279
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:48,080
They run on the same chip, use the same

280
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:50,280
model, and share the same forward pass

281
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:52,600
code, but they have completely opposite

282
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:55,440
resource profiles.

283
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:57,240
The first phase is called prefill.

284
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:59,640
The model takes the entire prompt at

285
00:10:57,240 --> 00:11:01,760
once and processes every token

286
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:03,000
simultaneously through every transformer

287
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:05,160
layer.

288
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:06,240
Attention runs across all positions in

289
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:08,480
parallel.

290
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:11,000
The feed forward network transforms each

291
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:12,280
vector independently, and by the time it

292
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,200
finishes,

293
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:16,040
the model has a representation for every

294
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:18,200
position and is ready to start

295
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:20,320
generating output.

296
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:22,080
The second phase is called decode, and

297
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:23,920
this is where the economics of inference

298
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:26,280
change completely.

299
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:27,480
Decode produces output tokens one at a

300
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:29,440
time.

301
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:31,800
To generate the first output token, the

302
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:34,440
model runs a full forward pass and

303
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:36,680
samples from the resulting distribution.

304
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:38,960
To generate the second, it runs another

305
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:41,440
complete forward pass.

306
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:44,120
The third token requires a third pass,

307
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:46,200
the fourth a fourth, and so on, until

308
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:48,360
the model emits a stop signal or [music]

309
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:50,240
hits a length limit.

310
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:53,760
Every single output token pays for its

311
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:55,520
own full trip through the entire model.

312
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:57,200
Now, we can look at these two phases

313
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:58,560
through the asymmetry we established

314
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:02,680
earlier.

315
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:05,280
Arithmetic is cheap, data is expensive.

316
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:07,440
In prefill, the model loads each layer's

317
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:09,640
weights from HBM into the arithmetic

318
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:11,920
units once, and that single weight

319
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:13,400
transfer gets used for arithmetic on

320
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:15,200
every token in the prompt

321
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:17,840
simultaneously.

322
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:20,440
For a 2,000 token prompt, every byte

323
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:23,280
pulled from memory gets used in roughly

324
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:25,720
2,000 multiply-accumulate operations

325
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:28,200
before the chip is done with it.

326
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:30,480
In decode, the model loads the exact

327
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:32,320
same weights, moves the exact same

328
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,520
number of bytes.

329
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:37,120
But all of that data movement serves a

330
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:40,040
single token of output.

331
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:41,720
One token generated, one full weight

332
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:44,360
transfer paid.

333
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:46,680
When serving a single request, the ratio

334
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:49,760
of useful arithmetic to bytes [music]

335
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:51,839
moved drops from around 2,000 to roughly

336
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:53,839
one.

337
00:12:51,839 --> 00:12:56,360
This is the structural fact that I think

338
00:12:53,839 --> 00:12:58,320
defines LLM inference.

339
00:12:56,360 --> 00:13:00,880
From the outside, prefill and decode

340
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:03,520
look like the same workload. Same model,

341
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:05,680
same chip, same code.

342
00:13:03,520 --> 00:13:07,720
But prefill gets thousands of arithmetic

343
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:10,880
operations out of every byte it moves

344
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:13,080
from memory, and decode gets one.

345
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:15,200
The memory bus pays the same toll in

346
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:17,440
both phases. [music] The payoff from

347
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:19,080
that toll differs by a factor of

348
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:21,160
thousands.

349
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:23,360
A natural first guess is that decode is

350
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:25,000
slow because the model is large, and

351
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:27,080
that guess is correct in a way that

352
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,800
misses the deeper point.

353
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:30,960
The model is exactly the same size in

354
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:34,040
both phases, and the bytes transferred

355
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:36,000
per forward pass are exactly the same.

356
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:38,280
What changes is how much useful

357
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,080
computation those transferred bytes

358
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:42,400
actually perform.

359
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:44,880
Prefill amortizes the cost of moving the

360
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:47,160
model across the bus over thousands of

361
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:49,360
tokens of parallel work.

362
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:51,320
Decode has no tokens to share the cost

363
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:53,200
with.

364
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:54,720
Next, we put real numbers on this and

365
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:56,600
place both phases on a standard

366
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:58,640
performance diagram, so we can be

367
00:13:56,600 --> 00:14:00,760
precise about how large the gap is and

368
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:02,680
what it costs.

369
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:04,920
To put numbers on the comparison between

370
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:08,520
prefill and decode, we need one piece of

371
00:14:04,920 --> 00:14:10,880
vocabulary, arithmetic intensity.

372
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:14,080
It measures how much work a chip gets

373
00:14:10,880 --> 00:14:16,520
out of each byte it drags from memory.

374
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:18,880
You take the total arithmetic operations

375
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:21,240
a workload performs and divide by the

376
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:24,400
total bytes it moves between off-chip

377
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:27,680
memory and the on-die compute units.

378
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:29,400
The result is operations per byte.

379
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:32,680
A workload with high arithmetic

380
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:35,400
intensity reuses each loaded byte many

381
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:38,080
times over, squeezing dozens or hundreds

382
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:39,760
of multiplies and adds out of it before

383
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:41,880
discarding it.

384
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:44,040
A workload with low arithmetic intensity

385
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:47,080
loads a byte, does almost nothing with

386
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:48,960
it, and immediately needs another.

387
00:14:47,080 --> 00:14:51,640
The whole point of the metric is to tell

388
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:53,200
you in advance whether a given workload

389
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:55,760
will be bottlenecked by the chip's

390
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:57,480
ability to do math or bytes' ability to

391
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:00,440
deliver bytes.

392
00:14:57,480 --> 00:15:02,920
Once we have arithmetic intensity,

393
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:05,280
there is a single diagram that tells us

394
00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:07,080
how any chip will execute [music] any

395
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:08,800
operation.

396
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:11,800
It's called the roofline model,

397
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:14,040
introduced in 2009 by Samuel Williams,

398
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:15,600
Andrew Waterman, and David Patterson at

399
00:15:14,040 --> 00:15:17,480
Berkeley.

400
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:18,920
The horizontal axis is arithmetic

401
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:21,040
intensity.

402
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:23,920
The vertical axis is achievable

403
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:25,960
performance in operations per second.

404
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:28,240
Both are logarithmic.

405
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:31,240
The diagram draws two ceilings and a

406
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:33,360
workload cannot exceed either one.

407
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:34,920
The first ceiling is flat.

408
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:37,200
The chip's peak floating point

409
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:39,640
throughput, the absolute maximum

410
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:43,440
operations per second the arithmetic

411
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:46,080
units can sustain when perfectly fed.

412
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:48,160
The second ceiling is slanted. It rises

413
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:51,040
from the lower left at a rate determined

414
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:52,800
by the chip's memory bandwidth.

415
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:55,280
At an arithmetic intensity of one

416
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:57,320
operation per byte with bandwidth of 3

417
00:15:55,280 --> 00:15:59,400
terabytes per second, the slanted

418
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:01,400
ceiling caps performance at 3 trillion

419
00:15:59,400 --> 00:16:03,920
operations per second.

420
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:06,600
At 10 operations per byte, it rises to

421
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:08,280
30 trillion. The slope is the bandwidth

422
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:10,200
itself.

423
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:11,600
Your workload hits whichever ceiling is

424
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:13,960
lower.

425
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:16,160
If arithmetic intensity is low, the

426
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:18,680
slanted bandwidth ceiling is the binding

427
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:21,000
constraint. If it's high, the flat

428
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:23,400
compute ceiling is.

429
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:26,560
These two lines meet at a single point

430
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:28,920
and that point has a name, the ridge.

431
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:30,920
It is the arithmetic intensity at which

432
00:16:28,920 --> 00:16:33,040
the chip transitions from being

433
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:35,680
bandwidth limited to being compute

434
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:38,800
limited. Below the ridge, we call the

435
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:40,280
workload memory bound. Above it, compute

436
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:42,360
bound.

437
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:44,480
The roof line is a simplification. It

438
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:47,320
assumes one memory level, ignores

439
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:50,400
caching, ignores launch latency, treats

440
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:52,680
every operation as perfectly pipelined.

441
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:54,520
But as a first order diagnostic for

442
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:57,320
whether a workload will starve the math

443
00:16:54,520 --> 00:17:01,839
units or starve the memory bus, it has

444
00:16:57,320 --> 00:17:04,120
held up remarkably well since 2009.

445
00:17:01,839 --> 00:17:07,560
We can plug in real numbers.

446
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:09,680
The Nvidia H100 peaks at 989 trillion

447
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:12,300
16-bit floating point operations per

448
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:15,600
second, just under 1 quadrillion. Its

449
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:17,280
[music] HBM bandwidth is 3.35 trillion

450
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:21,360
bytes per second.

451
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:24,120
Divide. 989 over 3.35.

452
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:26,520
The ridge lands at approximately 295

453
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:28,640
operations per byte.

454
00:17:26,520 --> 00:17:30,680
Any workload with arithmetic intensity

455
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:33,320
below 295

456
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:37,520
is memory bound on this chip. Anything

457
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:39,760
above 295 is compute bound.

458
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:41,840
Now we place prefill and decode on the

459
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:44,240
same diagram, and this is where the

460
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:46,520
picture gets striking.

461
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:49,440
For a 70 billion parameter model a

462
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:51,760
prompt of 2000 tokens, prefill performs

463
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:55,320
roughly 290 trillion arithmetic

464
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:57,560
operations and moves approximately 146

465
00:17:55,320 --> 00:18:00,720
gigabytes through HBM.

466
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:03,240
Mostly the 140 gigabyte weight pass plus

467
00:18:00,720 --> 00:18:05,040
a few gigabytes of activations and KV

468
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:09,560
cache writes.

469
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:11,600
290 trillion divided by 146 billion

470
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:15,600
gives an arithmetic intensity of

471
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:18,960
approximately 2000 operations per byte.

472
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:22,040
Against a ridge of 295,

473
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:23,760
that is about seven times to the right.

474
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:25,040
Prefill lives on the flat compute

475
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:27,720
ceiling.

476
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:30,960
Arithmetic units are saturated and the

477
00:18:27,720 --> 00:18:33,120
memory bus has bandwidth to spare.

478
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:36,080
For the same model, decoding one token

479
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:38,880
at batch size one performs roughly 140

480
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:41,320
billion arithmetic operations and moves

481
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:42,920
the same 140 gigabytes of weights

482
00:18:41,320 --> 00:18:46,760
through HBM.

483
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:48,120
140 billion divided by 140 billion,

484
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:51,760
the arithmetic intensity is

485
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:53,040
approximately one operation per byte.

486
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:57,080
One.

487
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:57,080
Against a ridge of 295,

488
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:01,400
that is nearly 300 times to the left of

489
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:03,880
the break-even point.

490
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:07,440
Decode sits deep on the slanted

491
00:19:03,880 --> 00:19:10,480
bandwidth ceiling with HBM saturated at

492
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:12,800
close to 100% utilization

493
00:19:10,480 --> 00:19:15,400
and the arithmetic units running at less

494
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:17,000
than 1%.

495
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:19,360
This is the structural answer to where

496
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,960
the 30 milliseconds went.

497
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:22,120
The chip is doing what it was built to

498
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:23,720
do.

499
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:24,880
The model is doing what it was built to

500
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:26,960
do.

501
00:19:24,880 --> 00:19:30,280
The workload structure simply forces the

502
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:32,720
chip into a regime where 99% of every

503
00:19:30,280 --> 00:19:34,440
decode step is spent waiting for bytes

504
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:37,800
to arrive from memory while the

505
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:40,080
arithmetic units sit idle.

506
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:42,520
And from the same numbers, we can derive

507
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:46,680
a hard throughput ceiling.

508
00:19:42,520 --> 00:19:50,160
Memory bandwidth is 3.35 TB per second.

509
00:19:46,680 --> 00:19:52,440
The model reads about 140 GB per output

510
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:54,760
token at batch size one.

511
00:19:52,440 --> 00:19:57,280
Divide bandwidth by bytes per token and

512
00:19:54,760 --> 00:20:00,120
you get the maximum decode rate. Roughly

513
00:19:57,280 --> 00:20:01,720
24 tokens per second.

514
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:03,400
That is a physical ceiling, not a

515
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:05,760
software one.

516
00:20:03,400 --> 00:20:07,480
No kernel optimization can exceed it

517
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:10,000
because the limit is just how fast the

518
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:11,880
memory bus can deliver weights.

519
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:15,360
The only ways past it are to raise the

520
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:17,280
bandwidth by moving to a different chip.

521
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:19,280
To shrink the bytes per token by storing

522
00:20:17,280 --> 00:20:21,040
the model in lower precision or

523
00:20:19,280 --> 00:20:22,800
compressing the runtime structures it

524
00:20:21,040 --> 00:20:24,560
has to read.

525
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:27,240
Or to amortize the weight transfer

526
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:29,600
across more tokens at a time by batching

527
00:20:27,240 --> 00:20:33,240
independent requests or generating

528
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:35,200
multiple tokens per forward pass.

529
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:37,360
We'll go deep into the three roots later

530
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:39,880
in this series.

531
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:42,920
We left one question unanswered earlier.

532
00:20:39,880 --> 00:20:45,560
When we set up prefill and decode.

533
00:20:42,920 --> 00:20:47,600
Why is decode sequential at all? Why can

534
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:48,444
the model not process its output tokens

535
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:48,680
in parallel

536
00:20:48,444 --> 00:20:51,800
>> [music]

537
00:20:48,680 --> 00:20:53,240
>> the way prefill processes the input?

538
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:55,800
The answer is in the attention

539
00:20:53,240 --> 00:20:58,240
mechanism, specifically in how attention

540
00:20:55,800 --> 00:21:00,680
depends on past tokens.

541
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:02,680
And the resolution to that dependency is

542
00:21:00,680 --> 00:21:05,000
a runtime data structure called the KV

543
00:21:02,680 --> 00:21:07,360
cache, which turns out to be the single

544
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:10,160
most consequential data structure in

545
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:12,480
production inference.

546
00:21:10,160 --> 00:21:14,600
Recall from earlier, in each transformer

547
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:17,360
layer, the attention step produces a

548
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:19,560
query, a key, and a value for every

549
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:21,400
token in the sequence.

550
00:21:19,560 --> 00:21:23,560
The attention output for a given token

551
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:25,200
is a weighted combination of values,

552
00:21:23,560 --> 00:21:27,600
where the weights come from comparing

553
00:21:25,200 --> 00:21:29,400
that token's query against the keys of

554
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:30,880
every other token.

555
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:33,040
The constraint is that attention is

556
00:21:30,880 --> 00:21:35,000
causal, meaning a token at position T

557
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:38,000
can only attend to tokens at positions

558
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:40,440
zero through T, never forward.

559
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:43,600
In prefill, every token of the prompt

560
00:21:40,440 --> 00:21:46,200
exists at the same time, so every query,

561
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:47,640
key, and value can be computed in

562
00:21:46,200 --> 00:21:50,560
parallel.

563
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:53,480
There is no sequential dependency.

564
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:55,280
In decode, the situation changes.

565
00:21:53,480 --> 00:21:57,840
When the model generates the token at

566
00:21:55,280 --> 00:22:00,480
position T, computing that token's

567
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:03,280
attention output requires the keys and

568
00:22:00,480 --> 00:22:06,390
values for every token at positions zero

569
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:06,720
through T minus one, the entire prefix,

570
00:22:06,390 --> 00:22:08,720
>> [music]

571
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:10,560
>> built from the original prompt plus

572
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:11,800
everything the model has generated so

573
00:22:10,560 --> 00:22:13,880
far.

574
00:22:11,800 --> 00:22:16,400
Those keys and values were computed from

575
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:19,040
the earlier tokens' representations as

576
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:21,400
they pass through each layer.

577
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:23,600
Here is the key observation,

578
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:26,120
and it is easy to miss on a first pass

579
00:22:23,600 --> 00:22:28,520
through the attention equations.

580
00:22:26,120 --> 00:22:31,400
The keys and values for positions zero

581
00:22:28,520 --> 00:22:33,880
through T minus one do not depend on the

582
00:22:31,400 --> 00:22:35,600
token at position T.

583
00:22:33,880 --> 00:22:37,880
They were fully determined when those

584
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:40,000
earlier tokens were processed.

585
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:42,360
They do not change as new tokens get

586
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:44,400
generated afterward.

587
00:22:42,360 --> 00:22:46,880
So, if we recomputed them from scratch

588
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:48,560
every time we generated a new token, we

589
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:50,680
would be doing the same work over and

590
00:22:48,560 --> 00:22:52,840
over, and the amount of redundant work

591
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:54,600
would grow with the sequence.

592
00:22:52,840 --> 00:22:57,480
By the time we are generating token

593
00:22:54,600 --> 00:23:01,360
1,000, producing that single token would

594
00:22:57,480 --> 00:23:03,880
mean recomputing keys and values for 999

595
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:05,080
prior positions through every layer of

596
00:23:03,880 --> 00:23:07,320
the model.

597
00:23:05,080 --> 00:23:09,840
The total work to generate end tokens

598
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:13,200
would be quadratic in n.

599
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:14,760
The fix is to save the keys and values.

600
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:17,640
Every time a token passes through a

601
00:23:14,760 --> 00:23:21,040
layer, the key and value it produces get

602
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:23,840
written into a dedicated region of HBM.

603
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:25,200
That region is the key value cache or KV

604
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:27,000
cache.

605
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:28,800
When we generate the next token, we

606
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,886
compute just that one token's key and

607
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:31,040
value, append them to the cache,

608
00:23:30,886 --> 00:23:32,720
>> [music]

609
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:34,120
>> and read the full cache to compute

610
00:23:32,720 --> 00:23:36,600
attention.

611
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:38,560
Linear work per token instead of

612
00:23:36,600 --> 00:23:41,480
quadratic.

613
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:44,320
This is an obvious optimization once you

614
00:23:41,480 --> 00:23:46,920
see it. Every modern serving system

615
00:23:44,320 --> 00:23:50,920
implements it. It is the reason decode

616
00:23:46,920 --> 00:23:53,080
is computationally tractable at all.

617
00:23:50,920 --> 00:23:55,440
But the same data structure,

618
00:23:53,080 --> 00:23:57,800
the straightforward caching trick, is

619
00:23:55,440 --> 00:24:01,240
also the reason every other optimization

620
00:23:57,800 --> 00:24:03,680
in the rest of this series exists.

621
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:05,840
We can make this concrete.

622
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:08,720
The KV cache stores two vectors per

623
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:10,440
token, the key and the value in every

624
00:24:08,720 --> 00:24:13,840
layer of the model.

625
00:24:10,440 --> 00:24:16,240
For Llama 3 70B with 80 transformer

626
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:18,840
layers and eight key value heads of

627
00:24:16,240 --> 00:24:21,520
dimension 128

628
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:23,840
stored in 16-bit precision,

629
00:24:21,520 --> 00:24:28,320
the per token cache size works out to 2

630
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:31,320
* 80 * 8 * 128 * 2 bytes, which is

631
00:24:28,320 --> 00:24:31,320
327,680

632
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:37,640
bytes, about 320 kilobytes per token.

633
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,520
That number scales linearly with

634
00:24:37,640 --> 00:24:42,360
conversation length.

635
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:45,280
A 1,000 token conversation means roughly

636
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:48,000
320 megabytes of cache.

637
00:24:45,280 --> 00:24:51,720
At 10,000 tokens, we are at 3.2

638
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:53,240
gigabytes, and at 100,000 tokens, 32

639
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:55,040
gigabytes,

640
00:24:53,240 --> 00:24:58,160
which is already a significant fraction

641
00:24:55,040 --> 00:25:00,400
of the model's own 141 GB weight

642
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:03,120
footprint.

643
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,640
The cache lives in HBM right alongside

644
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,720
the weights.

645
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:08,800
Every decode step has to read every

646
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:11,440
weight as we established in the roofline

647
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:13,920
scene and also read every entry of the

648
00:25:11,440 --> 00:25:16,520
cache up to the current position.

649
00:25:13,920 --> 00:25:19,040
Both write the same memory bus drawing

650
00:25:16,520 --> 00:25:20,680
from the same bandwidth budget.

651
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:22,720
The bigger the cache grows, the more

652
00:25:20,680 --> 00:25:25,440
bytes per token and the lower the

653
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:27,480
throughput ceiling we derived earlier.

654
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:29,400
The model itself and the conversational

655
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:32,440
state of the request being served

656
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:34,120
compete for the same bus.

657
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:35,720
This data structure shapes every

658
00:25:34,120 --> 00:25:37,520
inference technique that comes after

659
00:25:35,720 --> 00:25:39,200
this video.

660
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:41,240
Some of those techniques shrink the

661
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:43,240
cache directly by changing how keys and

662
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:45,000
values are represented while others

663
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:47,120
share the cache across requests that

664
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:49,080
have a common prefix.

665
00:25:47,120 --> 00:25:51,400
Others split the cache across machines

666
00:25:49,080 --> 00:25:53,720
so that no single GPU has to hold the

667
00:25:51,400 --> 00:25:55,640
whole thing or refuse to materialize

668
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:57,240
parts of it at all.

669
00:25:55,640 --> 00:25:59,160
And some accept that a single

670
00:25:57,240 --> 00:26:01,640
conversation cannot be sped up past a

671
00:25:59,160 --> 00:26:04,120
certain point and instead amortize the

672
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:06,440
cost across many conversations running

673
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:07,960
at the same time.

674
00:26:06,440 --> 00:26:10,400
Everything so far has been from the

675
00:26:07,960 --> 00:26:12,680
chip's perspective. Bytes on a memory

676
00:26:10,400 --> 00:26:14,600
bus, arithmetic intensity, weight

677
00:26:12,680 --> 00:26:17,440
transfers.

678
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:19,200
The user does not see any of that.

679
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:22,320
When you type a question into a chat

680
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:24,720
interface and hit enter, you experience

681
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:27,360
two things in sequence.

682
00:26:24,720 --> 00:26:29,560
First, a pause. Half a second if the

683
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:30,960
prompt is short, several seconds if it

684
00:26:29,560 --> 00:26:32,920
is long.

685
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:35,880
Then, the first character of the

686
00:26:32,920 --> 00:26:38,600
response appears and from that point on,

687
00:26:35,880 --> 00:26:41,240
characters stream in at a steady cadence

688
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:44,080
until the model finishes.

689
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:47,440
That initial pause has a name.

690
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:49,520
Time to first token or TTFT. It measures

691
00:26:47,440 --> 00:26:52,160
the latency from submission to the first

692
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:55,080
visible token of the response.

693
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:56,760
TTFT is governed by the pre-fill phase

694
00:26:55,080 --> 00:26:59,280
because the model has to process the

695
00:26:56,760 --> 00:27:01,880
entire prompt through every layer before

696
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:03,640
it can produce any output. [music]

697
00:27:01,880 --> 00:27:05,080
The streaming cadence that follows has a

698
00:27:03,640 --> 00:27:07,800
name, too.

699
00:27:05,080 --> 00:27:10,080
Time per output token, abbreviated TPT,

700
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:11,600
sometimes called inter-token latency or

701
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:13,800
ITL.

702
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:16,400
It is the gap between successive output

703
00:27:13,800 --> 00:27:18,520
tokens governed by the decode phase

704
00:27:16,400 --> 00:27:21,400
because each output token requires its

705
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:23,840
own complete forward pass.

706
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:26,840
TTFT controls whether a product feels

707
00:27:23,840 --> 00:27:28,520
responsive. TPT controls whether the

708
00:27:26,840 --> 00:27:30,600
text arrives fast enough to read

709
00:27:28,520 --> 00:27:33,000
comfortably or stutters in a way that

710
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:34,640
breaks concentration.

711
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,840
These are the two latency numbers the

712
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:38,560
user actually feels and they are

713
00:27:36,840 --> 00:27:40,600
produced by phases with opposite

714
00:27:38,560 --> 00:27:43,680
resource profiles.

715
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:45,480
Improving TTFT is a compute problem.

716
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:46,920
Keep the arithmetic units fed during

717
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:49,600
pre-fill.

718
00:27:46,920 --> 00:27:51,880
Improving TPT is a bandwidth problem.

719
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:54,400
Reduce the bytes per token decode has to

720
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:55,720
move across the memory bus.

721
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:58,360
Different bottlenecks, different

722
00:27:55,720 --> 00:28:00,160
engineering levers.

723
00:27:58,360 --> 00:28:03,120
From the operator's side, there is a

724
00:28:00,160 --> 00:28:04,920
third number, throughput. Total tokens

725
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:06,920
generated per second across all

726
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:08,600
concurrent users.

727
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:11,040
They pay for the chip by the hour and

728
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:14,160
sell the tokens, so throughput is how

729
00:28:11,040 --> 00:28:15,680
they track whether the economics work.

730
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:18,160
But throughput by itself can be

731
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:20,320
misleading and this is the part I think

732
00:28:18,160 --> 00:28:22,600
matters most.

733
00:28:20,320 --> 00:28:24,560
There is a fourth metric called goodput

734
00:28:22,600 --> 00:28:27,360
and it is the one that actually decides

735
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:29,640
whether a serving system is viable.

736
00:28:27,360 --> 00:28:32,280
Goodput is not a separate measurement so

737
00:28:29,640 --> 00:28:34,040
much as a filter applied to throughput.

738
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:34,809
It counts only the tokens that were

739
00:28:34,040 --> 00:28:34,880
delivered

740
00:28:34,809 --> 00:28:36,800
>> [music]

741
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:38,120
>> within the system's promised latency

742
00:28:36,800 --> 00:28:40,000
targets.

743
00:28:38,120 --> 00:28:41,840
The ones that arrived fast enough that

744
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:43,440
the user did not give up and close the

745
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:45,480
tab.

746
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:47,760
You can run a system at enormous

747
00:28:45,480 --> 00:28:50,200
throughput by packing thousands of

748
00:28:47,760 --> 00:28:52,480
requests into large batches and

749
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:54,800
processing them slowly.

750
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:57,040
The tokens get generated. The throughput

751
00:28:54,800 --> 00:28:59,040
counter looks impressive.

752
00:28:57,040 --> 00:29:01,400
But if every user is staring at a blank

753
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:03,880
screen for 40 seconds before their first

754
00:29:01,400 --> 00:29:06,960
character appears, every one of those

755
00:29:03,880 --> 00:29:09,640
tokens has failed its TTFT target and

756
00:29:06,960 --> 00:29:11,880
good put is zero.

757
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:14,760
The system is producing tokens nobody

758
00:29:11,880 --> 00:29:17,000
was willing to wait for.

759
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:19,280
Good put, not raw throughput, is the

760
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:21,640
number every production serving system

761
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:23,560
is optimizing for.

762
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:26,200
Every architectural decision in the rest

763
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:28,400
of this series is, in some form, an

764
00:29:26,200 --> 00:29:31,600
attempt to push good put higher without

765
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:34,000
violating the latency contract.

766
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:36,400
Let's recap where we are.

767
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:39,040
Modern AI accelerators have a structural

768
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:42,240
asymmetry. Compute throughput is scaling

769
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:44,720
faster than off-chip memory bandwidth.

770
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:47,200
A transformer runs by streaming all of

771
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:50,880
its weights from HBM into the chip's

772
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:53,480
arithmetic units once per forward pass.

773
00:29:50,880 --> 00:29:55,000
A single inference request runs in two

774
00:29:53,480 --> 00:29:57,520
phases.

775
00:29:55,000 --> 00:30:00,000
Prefill is compute bound.

776
00:29:57,520 --> 00:30:02,880
Decode is memory bandwidth bound by a

777
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:04,960
factor of nearly 300.

778
00:30:02,880 --> 00:30:07,280
The KV cache, which makes decode

779
00:30:04,960 --> 00:30:10,000
tractable in the first place, lives in

780
00:30:07,280 --> 00:30:12,240
HBM alongside the weights and adds to

781
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:15,640
the bytes per token bill.

782
00:30:12,240 --> 00:30:18,960
TTFT and TBot are what the user feels.

783
00:30:15,640 --> 00:30:20,840
Good put is what the operator optimizes.

784
00:30:18,960 --> 00:30:22,960
The factor of 300 between what the

785
00:30:20,840 --> 00:30:25,120
arithmetic implies and what the chip

786
00:30:22,960 --> 00:30:27,800
actually delivers is not a mystery

787
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:30,640
anymore. It is bandwidth divided by

788
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:32,600
bytes per token.

789
00:30:30,640 --> 00:30:34,440
Now we know where the time goes. The

790
00:30:32,600 --> 00:30:35,880
rest of the series is about what to do

791
00:30:34,440 --> 00:30:38,600
about it.

792
00:30:35,880 --> 00:30:41,080
We open up the accelerator hardware,

793
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:43,000
look at the code that runs on it,

794
00:30:41,080 --> 00:30:45,440
and see what happens when you store

795
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:46,920
weights in fewer bits.

796
00:30:45,440 --> 00:30:49,840
From there,

797
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:52,360
splitting models across many chips,

798
00:30:49,840 --> 00:30:54,480
building models that only use part of

799
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:56,920
themselves per token,

800
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:58,640
production serving at scale,

801
00:30:56,920 --> 00:31:01,920
and a technique for exploiting idle

802
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:04,480
arithmetic units during decode.

803
00:31:01,920 --> 00:31:08,240
Then we trace one real request end to

804
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:08,240
end through the full stack.

